---
import AppsLayout from "@layouts/AppsLayout.astro";
import Header from "@components/audiorouter/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@components/Footer.astro";
import { withBase } from '@utils/paths';
---

<AppsLayout title="Chatty Sparrow - How to Send Processed Logic Pro Audio to Zoom on Mac" faviconPath={withBase("/favicons/audiorouter")} description="Route processed Logic Pro audio to Zoom on Mac using Audio Router on macOS.">

<Header />

<main>
    <section id="tutorial" class="features section">
      <div class="container section-title" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
        <div class="text-start">
          <article class="tutorial">
            <header>
              <p class="tutorial-category">Audio Router Tutorial</p>

              <h1>How to Send Processed Logic Pro Audio to Zoom on Mac</h1>

              <p class="tutorial-introduction">
                Use Audio Router to send a microphone, software instrument, backing
                track, or complete Logic Pro mix into Zoom as a virtual microphone.
                This lets Zoom participants hear audio after it has been processed by
                Logic Pro plug-ins such as EQ and compression.
              </p>
            </header>

            <aside class="tutorial-note" aria-labelledby="when-to-use-heading">
              <br/>
              <h2 id="when-to-use-heading">When should you use this setup?</h2>

              <p>
                You do not need Audio Router for an ordinary Zoom voice call. If you only
                need to speak through an unprocessed microphone, select that microphone
                directly in Zoom.
              </p>

              <p>Use this workflow when you want Zoom to receive:</p>

              <ul>
                <li>A microphone processed with Logic Pro plug-ins</li>
                <li>EQ, compression, gating, reverb, or other effects</li>
                <li>A software instrument or live instrument input</li>
                <li>Music, backing tracks, or sound effects</li>
                <li>Multiple Logic Pro tracks mixed together</li>
                <li>The complete output of a Logic Pro project</li>
              </ul>
            </aside>

            <section aria-labelledby="requirements-heading">
              <h2 id="requirements-heading">What you’ll need</h2>

              <ul>
                <li>A Mac running macOS 15 or later</li>
                <li>Audio Router installed and configured</li>
                <li>Logic Pro</li>
                <li>The Zoom desktop application</li>
                <li>A microphone or audio interface</li>
                <li>A headset or headphones</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="signal-flow-heading">
              <h2 id="signal-flow-heading">How the signal flows</h2>

              <pre aria-label="Logic Pro to Zoom audio signal flow">
          <code>Microphone or instrument
                    ↓
                Logic Pro
         EQ / compression / effects
                    ↓
               Audio Router
                    ↓
             Zoom microphone</code></pre>

              <p>
                Logic Pro processes the live input and sends its complete output to an
                Audio Router virtual device. Zoom uses that device as its microphone.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="logic-track-heading">
              <h2 id="logic-track-heading">1. Create a microphone track in Logic Pro</h2>

              <ol>
                <li>Open <strong>Logic Pro</strong>.</li>
                <li>Create a new <strong>Empty Project</strong>.</li>
                <li>Select <strong>Audio</strong> as the new track type.</li>
                <li>Select your microphone or audio interface as the input device.</li>
                <li>
                  Choose the appropriate mono input, commonly <strong>Input 1</strong>.
                </li>
                <li>Create the track.</li>
                <li>
                  Click the track’s <strong>R</strong> button to record-enable it.
                </li>
                <li>
                  Speak into the microphone and confirm that the track meter responds.
                </li>
              </ol>

              <aside class="tutorial-tip">
                <p>
                  You do not need to press Logic Pro’s main Record button. The track only
                  needs to be record-enabled so it can receive the live microphone
                  signal.
                </p>
              </aside>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="processing-heading">
              <h2 id="processing-heading">2. Add EQ and compression</h2>

              <ol>
                <li>Select the microphone track.</li>
                <li>Find an empty <strong>Audio FX</strong> slot.</li>
                <li>Add <strong>EQ → Channel EQ</strong>.</li>
                <li>In the next Audio FX slot, add <strong>Dynamics → Compressor</strong>.</li>
                <li>Use the mono versions when available.</li>
                <li>Speak and confirm that the track meter continues to respond.</li>
              </ol>

              <pre aria-label="Logic Pro microphone processing order">
          <code>Microphone
              ↓
          Channel EQ
              ↓
          Compressor
              ↓
          Logic Pro output</code></pre>

              <p>
                Default settings are sufficient for testing. You can adjust the EQ and
                compressor after confirming that the complete route works.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="router-session-heading">
              <h2 id="router-session-heading">3. Create an Audio Router session</h2>

              <ol>
                <li>Open <strong>Audio Router</strong>.</li>
                <li>
                  Click the large <strong>+</strong> button at the bottom of the session
                  sidebar.
                </li>
                <li>
                  Rename the new session <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong>.
                </li>
                <li>
                  Confirm that the session is enabled and its status indicator is green.
                </li>
                <li>Keep the default <strong>Pass-Through</strong> source.</li>
                <li>
                  Confirm that the Pass-Through source is connected to Channels 1 and 2.
                </li>
              </ol>

              <pre aria-label="Audio Router channel connections">
            <code>Pass-Through Left  → Channel 1
            Pass-Through Right → Channel 2</code></pre>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="logic-output-heading">
              <h2 id="logic-output-heading">
                4. Send Logic Pro’s output to Audio Router
              </h2>

              <ol>
                <li>Return to Logic Pro.</li>
                <li>Open <strong>Logic Pro → Settings → Audio</strong>.</li>
                <li>Open the <strong>Devices</strong> section.</li>
                <li>Set <strong>Output Device</strong> to <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong>.</li>
                <li>Keep your microphone or audio interface selected as Logic Pro’s input device.</li>
                <li>Apply the audio-device change if Logic Pro asks.</li>
                <li>Return to the microphone track and keep it record-enabled.</li>
                <li>Speak into the microphone.</li>
                <li>Confirm that Audio Router’s Pass-Through and Channels meters respond.</li>
              </ol>

              <aside class="tutorial-note">
                <p>
                  In our test, enabling the track’s <strong>R</strong> button was
                  sufficient to pass the microphone through Logic Pro. Depending on your
                  Logic Pro monitoring settings, you may also need to enable the track’s <strong>I</strong> Input Monitoring button.
                </p>
              </aside>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="zoom-heading">
              <h2 id="zoom-heading">5. Select the Audio Router device in Zoom</h2>

              <ol>
                <li>Open the Zoom desktop application.</li>
                <li>Open <strong>Settings → Audio</strong>.</li>
                <li>Under <strong>Microphone</strong>, select <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong>.</li>
                <li>Under <strong>Speaker</strong>, select your headset or headphones.</li>
                <li>Start Zoom’s microphone test.</li>
                <li>Speak into the microphone.</li>
                <li>Confirm that Zoom’s microphone input meter responds and that the test playback contains your voice.</li>
              </ol>

              <div class="tutorial-settings-summary">
                <h3>Recommended Zoom settings</h3>

                <dl>
                  <div>
                    <dt>Microphone</dt>
                    <dd>Logic to Zoom</dd>
                  </div>

                  <div>
                    <dt>Speaker</dt>
                    <dd>Your headset or headphones</dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
              </div>

              <p>Do not select <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong> as Zoom’s speaker. Incoming Zoom audio should go directly to your headset.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="verify-heading">
              <h2 id="verify-heading">6. Verify that Zoom is receiving Logic Pro</h2>

              <p>Perform this short test to confirm that Zoom is not receiving the microphone through another path:</p>

              <ol>
                <li>Keep Zoom’s microphone test running.</li>
                <li>Mute the microphone track in Logic Pro.</li>
                <li>Speak into the microphone.</li>
                <li>Confirm that Zoom’s input meter stops responding.</li>
                <li>Unmute the Logic Pro track.</li>
                <li>Speak again and confirm that Zoom’s meter responds.</li>
              </ol>

              <p>
                If muting the Logic Pro track stops the Zoom signal, the microphone is
                successfully passing through Logic Pro and its plug-in chain.
              </p>

              <p>
                You can also bypass and re-enable Channel EQ or Compressor while testing.
                The change heard in Zoom confirms that Logic Pro’s processed output is
                reaching the call.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="monitoring-heading">
              <h2 id="monitoring-heading">7. Optional: Monitor the processed audio</h2>

              <p>
                Monitoring is not required for sending Logic Pro audio into Zoom. If you
                want to hear the processed Logic Pro output yourself:
              </p>

              <ol>
                <li>Open the <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong> Audio Router session.</li>
                <li>Click the <span class="fs-5s"><i class="bi bi-plus-circle"></i></span> button beside <strong>Monitor</strong>.</li>
                <li>Select your headset.</li>
                <li>Confirm that Channels 1 and 2 connect to the headset’s left and right inputs.</li>
                <li>Begin with the monitor level very low.</li>
                <li>Raise the level gradually while speaking.</li>
              </ol>

              <aside class="tutorial-warning">
                <p>
                  <strong>Monitoring latency:</strong> Software monitoring may produce a short delay between speaking and hearing yourself. This can sound like an echo. If the delay is
                  distracting, remove or mute the Audio Router monitor. Zoom will continue receiving the Logic Pro output.
                </p>
              </aside>

              <p>
                Always use headphones for live microphone monitoring. Speakers can allow monitored audio to re-enter the microphone and cause acoustic feedback.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="level-heading">
              <h2 id="level-heading">8. Adjust the microphone level</h2>

              <p>If your Zoom recording sounds too quiet, adjust the level before it reaches Zoom:</p>

              <ul>
                <li>Raise the microphone track fader slightly in Logic Pro.</li>
                <li>Adjust the compressor’s makeup or output gain.</li>
                <li>If using an audio interface, check its physical input-gain control.</li>
                <li>Watch the meters while speaking at your normal volume.</li>
              </ul>

              <p>
                Avoid sustained red meter levels. Raising the signal too far can cause clipping and distortion that Zoom cannot repair.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="recording-test-heading">
              <h2 id="recording-test-heading">9. Make a Zoom test recording</h2>

              <ol>
                <li>Start a private Zoom meeting as the host.</li>
                <li>Confirm that <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong> is still selected as the microphone.</li>
                <li>In the meeting controls, choose <strong>Record</strong>. If it is not visible, open <strong>More (…) → Record</strong>.</li>
                <li>Select <strong>Record to this Computer</strong>.</li>
                <li>Speak normally for several seconds.</li>
                <li>Bypass the compressor and speak again.</li>
                <li>Re-enable the compressor and speak again.</li>
                <li>Mute and unmute the Logic Pro track to verify the signal path.</li>
                <li>Stop the recording and end the meeting.</li>
                <li>Listen to the processed recording.</li>
              </ol>

              <p>Zoom normally stores computer recordings in the <strong>Documents/Zoom</strong> folder.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="troubleshooting-heading">
              <h2 id="troubleshooting-heading">Troubleshooting</h2>

              <h3>Logic Pro is not receiving the microphone</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Confirm that the correct microphone input is selected.</li>
                <li>Click the track’s <strong>R</strong> button.</li>
                <li>If necessary, enable the track’s <strong>I</strong> Input Monitoring button.</li>
                <li>Check that macOS has granted Logic Pro microphone permission.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Audio Router receives no signal from Logic Pro</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Confirm that Logic Pro’s Output Device is <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong>.</li>
                <li>Make sure the Logic Pro track is record-enabled and unmuted.</li>
                <li>Confirm that Pass-Through is connected to Audio Router Channels 1 and 2.</li>
                <li>Make sure the Audio Router session is enabled and unmuted.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Audio Router meters move, but Zoom receives nothing</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Confirm that Zoom’s Microphone is set to <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong>.</li>
                <li>Restart Zoom to refresh its audio-device list.</li>
                <li>Check the Zoom microphone input level.</li>
                <li>Confirm that the Logic Pro track is not muted.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Your voice sounds delayed in your headset</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Remove or mute the Audio Router monitor.</li>
                <li>Continue using Zoom with monitoring disabled.</li>
                <li>If available, use direct hardware monitoring for lower latency, noting that direct monitoring normally plays the unprocessed microphone.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Your voice is too quiet</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Raise the Logic Pro track fader gradually.</li>
                <li>Adjust the compressor’s output gain.</li>
                <li>Increase the microphone preamp gain if available.</li>
                <li>Avoid clipping in Logic Pro and Audio Router.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="final-configuration-heading">
              <h2 id="final-configuration-heading">Final configuration</h2>

              <h3>Logic Pro</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Input Device: <strong>Your microphone or interface</strong></li>
                <li>Output Device: <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong></li>
                <li>Microphone track: <strong>Record-enabled and unmuted</strong></li>
                <li>Audio FX: <strong>Channel EQ and Compressor</strong></li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Audio Router</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Session: <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong></li>
                <li>Source: <strong>Pass-Through</strong></li>
                <li>Channels: <strong>Stereo</strong></li>
                <li>Monitor: <strong>Optional headset monitoring</strong></li>
                <li>Session status: <strong>Enabled and unmuted</strong></li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Zoom</h3>

              <ul>
                <li>Microphone: <strong>Logic to Zoom</strong></li>
                <li>Speaker: <strong>Your headset or headphones</strong></li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <footer class="tutorial-footer">
              <p>
                This setup sends Logic Pro’s complete output into Zoom. In addition to a
                processed microphone, you can use it to share software instruments,
                backing tracks, live instrument inputs, sound effects, or a complete DAW
                mix with meeting participants.
              </p>
            </footer>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</main>

<Footer />

</AppsLayout>